\begin{abstract}
We present an adaptation of the quantum random walk (QRW) 2-sieve of Chailloux and Loyer \cite{CL21} to serve as the inner two-list subroutine inside the quantum 4-sieve framework of Chailloux and Loyer \cite{CL23}. The adaptation preserves the Johnson graph, the second-level random product code, and the quantum data structure of the original algorithm, and modifies only the marked condition and the probability that a random cross-pair is a solution. We provide the complete algorithms, discuss the modifications required, and give a full complexity analysis for the running time, QRAM, quantum memory, and classical memory. We optimise the QRW parameters for the bipartite setting and show that the optimal choice is \(c_1 \approx 0.13\), giving \(T_{12} = T_{34} = 2^{0.139d+o(d)}\). The resulting algorithm yields a new time-memory trade-off for the Shortest Vector Problem (SVP).
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Shortest Vector Problem (SVP) is at the foundation of lattice-based cryptography. The best known heuristic quantum algorithm for SVP runs in time \(2^{0.2570d+o(d)}\) \cite{CL21}, obtained by replacing Grover's algorithm inside the 2-sieve by a quantum random walk with an additional layer of locality-sensitive filtering. In parallel, \(k\)-sieve algorithms have been developed to reduce memory requirements at the cost of increased running time \cite{CL23}. In particular, the quantum 4-sieve of \cite{CL23} uses nested Grover searches and achieves a time-memory trade-off with quantum-accessible classical memory of size \(2^{0.1724d}\) to \(2^{0.1813d}\).

In this work, we investigate whether the QRW 2-sieve of \cite{CL21} can be used as the inner two-list subroutine inside the 4-sieve framework of \cite{CL23}, replacing the Grover-based inner 2-sieve. This requires adapting the QRW 2-sieve to handle two distinct lists rather than one, an arbitrary configuration threshold \(C'_{12}(\alpha)\) rather than the fixed angle \(\theta_\alpha^*\), and possibly non-unit vectors in the second-level call.

We show that the core QRW structure remains unchanged: the Johnson graph, the second random product code \(\mathcal{C}_2\), the quantum data structure for the lists \(J^v(\vec{t})\), and the MNRS framework are all preserved. Only the marked condition and the probability \(p_{\mathrm{sol}}\) that a random cross-pair is a solution must be adjusted. We give the complete algorithms, discuss the modifications, and provide the full complexity analysis. We also optimise the QRW parameters for the bipartite setting, finding that the optimal choice is \(c = 0.3\), \(c_1 \approx 0.13\), \(c_2 = 0\), \(\rho = 0\) for the first two inner calls, yielding \(T_{12} = T_{34} = 2^{0.139d+o(d)}\).

\section{Preliminaries and Notation}

We use the notation of \cite{CL21,CL23}. Let \(d\) be the lattice dimension and define the fundamental list-size parameter
\begin{equation}
N \coloneqq \left(\sqrt{4/3}\right)^d = 2^{0.2075d+o(d)}.
\end{equation}
This quantity \(N\) is exponential in \(d\) and serves as the reference scale for all exponents in the algorithm. A list of size \(N^{a}\) contains \(2^{a\cdot 0.2075d + o(d)}\) elements; throughout the paper we write \(N^{a+o(1)}\) to denote such a list, absorbing subexponential factors into the \(o(1)\).

For an angle \(\alpha \in [0,\pi/2]\), the volume ratio of a spherical cap of angle \(\alpha\) is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}_d(\alpha) \coloneqq \mathrm{poly}(d)\sin^d(\alpha),
\end{equation}
which represents the fraction of the unit sphere covered by a cap of angular radius \(\alpha\). For two angles \(\alpha\) and \(\theta\), the volume ratio of a wedge formed by two caps of angle \(\alpha\) with centers separated by angle \(\theta\) is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{W}_d(\alpha,\theta) \coloneqq \mathrm{poly}(d)\left(1-\frac{2\cos^2(\alpha)}{1+\cos(\theta)}\right)^{d/2},
\end{equation}
which is well-defined provided \(2\cos^2(\alpha) < 1+\cos(\theta)\), i.e., the two caps overlap.

The critical angle between residual vectors that characterises reducibility of two border points is
\begin{equation}
\theta_\alpha^* \coloneqq 2\arcsin\!\left(\frac{1}{2\sin(\alpha)}\right),
\end{equation}
as established in \cite[Proposition~4]{CL21}. Two residual vectors \(\vec{y}_0,\vec{y}_1\) on the border of a filter of angle \(\alpha\) are reducible if and only if \(\theta(\vec{y}_0,\vec{y}_1) \le \theta_\alpha^*\).

\paragraph{Outer 4-sieve parameters.} The outer 4-sieve uses a prefiltering angle \(\alpha_0 \in [\pi/3,\pi/2]\) and a configuration matrix \(C \in \mathbb{R}^{4\times 4}\). The reduction to residual vectors is denoted \(C'(\alpha_0)\), with entries \(C'_{ij}(\alpha_0)\) given by \cite[Lemma~1]{CL23}:
\begin{equation}
C'_{ij}(\alpha_0) = \frac{1}{\sin^2(\alpha_0)}\left(C_{ij} + \frac{\cos^2(\alpha_0)}{3}\right) \quad (i \neq j).
\end{equation}
The effective threshold for the second-level 2-sieve that builds \(L_{1234}\) is
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathrm{eff}} \coloneqq \frac{\sin^2(\alpha_0)}{2R^2} - 1, \qquad R^2 \coloneqq 2 + 2C_{12},
\end{equation}
as given in \cite[Lemma~6]{CL23}.

\paragraph{QRW parameters.} The adapted QRW 2-sieve uses four free parameters \(c, c_1, c_2, \rho\) satisfying \(0 \le c_2 \le c_1 \le c \le 1\) and \(0 \le \rho \le \rho_0\). From these, the following derived quantities are defined. The angle \(\alpha\) (not to be confused with \(\alpha_0\)) satisfies \(\mathcal{V}_d(\alpha) = N^{-(1-c)}\). The angle \(\beta\) of the second random product code satisfies \(\mathcal{V}_d(\beta) = N^{c_2-c_1}\). The target angle is \(\theta' \coloneqq \arccos(C')\). The collision parameter \(\rho_0\) is defined by \(N^{\rho_0} \coloneqq \mathcal{V}_d(\beta)/\mathcal{W}_d(\beta,\theta')\). The solution probability is
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{sol}} \coloneqq \frac{1}{2}\bigl(1-(C')^2\bigr)^{d/2},
\label{eq:psol}
\end{equation}
where the factor \(1/2\) accounts for the bipartite nature of the problem (a random pair from the combined list is a cross-pair with probability \(1/2\)). The expected number of solutions per filter is \(N^\zeta \coloneqq N^{2c}\cdot p_{\mathrm{sol}}\).

The QRW costs are the setup cost \(\mathcal{S} \coloneqq N^{c_1+\rho}\), the update cost \(\mathcal{U} \coloneqq N^{\max\{\rho,(\rho+c_2)/2\}}\), and the checking cost \(\mathcal{C} \coloneqq 1\). The spectral gap of the Johnson graph is \(\delta \coloneqq N^{-c_1}\). The fraction of marked vertices is
\begin{equation}
\epsilon \coloneqq N^{2c_1}\cdot p_{\mathrm{sol}}\cdot N^{\rho-\rho_0}.
\end{equation}

\section{Modified QRW 2-Sieve}

\subsection{Preserved components}

The adaptation preserves the following components of the QRW 2-sieve of \cite{CL21} without structural modification. The Johnson graph \(J(N, N^{c_1})\) on the combined list, with spectral gap \(\delta = N^{-c_1}\) and the MNRS quantum walk framework, is retained exactly. The second random product code \(\mathcal{C}_2\) with angle \(\beta\) satisfying \(\mathcal{V}_d(\beta) = N^{c_2-c_1}\) and size \(|\mathcal{C}_2| = N^{\rho+c_1-c_2}\) is constructed identically. The quantum data structure for the lists \(J^v(\vec{t}) = f_\beta(\vec{t})\cap L^v\), supporting insertions and deletions in quantum superposition, is used as in the original algorithm. The setup, update, and check cost formulas are formally unchanged.

\subsection{Required modifications}

Three modifications are required to adapt the QRW 2-sieve to the bipartite setting. First, we form the combined list \(L = R_A \cup R_B\) and tag each element by a bit indicating its origin, with \(0\) for \(R_A\) and \(1\) for \(R_B\). The Johnson graph is built on \(L\) with \(|L| = 2N_R\). This tagging is a standard technique for bipartite search problems and does not affect the graph structure. Second, the marked condition is redefined: a vertex \(v\) is marked if there exist \(\vec{t} \in \mathcal{C}_2\) and distinct \(\vec{y}_i,\vec{y}_j \in \tilde{J}^v(\vec{t})\) such that \(\vec{y}_i\) and \(\vec{y}_j\) have different tags and \(\langle \vec{y}_i|\vec{y}_j\rangle \le C'\). Third, the solution probability acquires the factor \(1/2\) as given in Equation~\eqref{eq:psol}.

The factor \(1/2\) in \(p_{\mathrm{sol}}\) is the only structural change to the probability. It propagates through \(\epsilon\) and \(\zeta\) but does not affect the setup, update, or check costs, since the number of opposite-tag elements in a vertex is still \(\Theta(N^{c_1})\).

\subsection{Adapted algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Adapted QRW 2-Sieve for bipartite configuration problems}
\label{alg:qrw}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require two residual lists \(R_A, R_B\) of size \(N_R\) each; threshold \(C'\); QRW parameters \(c, c_1, c_2, \rho\).
\Ensure all pairs \((\vec{y}_a, \vec{y}_b) \in R_A \times R_B\) with \(\langle \vec{y}_a|\vec{y}_b\rangle \le C'\).
\State Form \(L \coloneqq R_A \cup R_B\) with tags.
\State Compute \(\theta' \coloneqq \arccos(C')\) and \(p_{\mathrm{sol}} \coloneqq \frac{1}{2}(1-C'^2)^{d/2}\).
\State Sample random product code \(\mathcal{C}_2\) with \(|\mathcal{C}_2| = N^{\rho+c_1-c_2}\) and angle \(\beta\) satisfying \(\mathcal{V}_d(\beta) = N^{c_2-c_1}\).
\State Build Johnson graph \(J(N, N^{c_1})\) on \(L\).
\For{each vertex \(v\)}
    \State Compute \(J^v(\vec{t}) = f_\beta(\vec{t}) \cap L^v\) for each \(\vec{t}\in\mathcal{C}_2\).
    \State Truncate to \(\tilde{J}^v(\vec{t})\) of size \(\le 2N^{c_2}\).
    \State Set marked bit \(M(v)\) according to the marked condition above.
\EndFor
\While{total solutions found \(< N^\zeta/2\)}
    \State Pick a new random product code \(\mathcal{C}_2\).
    \While{solutions found with this \(\mathcal{C}_2\) \(< N^{\zeta+\rho-\rho_0}/2\)}
        \State Run the MNRS quantum walk to find one marked vertex.
    \EndWhile
\EndWhile
\State \Return all collected pairs.
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Main Algorithm: 4-Sieve with QRW Inner 2-Sieve}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{4-Sieve with QRW inner 2-sieve}
\label{alg:main}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require list \(L\) of \(N' = N^{1+o(1)}\) lattice vectors of norm \(1\); reducing factor \(\gamma < 1\); prefiltering angle \(\alpha_0\); configuration \(C\in\mathbb{R}^{4\times 4}\); QRW parameters.
\Ensure list \(L'\) of \(N'\) lattice vectors of norm \(\le \gamma\).
\State \(L' \coloneqq \emptyset\).
\While{\(|L'| \le N'\)}
    \State Sample a 4-RPC \(\mathfrak{C}\) with codewords \((\mathbf{A}_1^j,\mathbf{A}_2^j,\mathbf{A}_3^j,\mathbf{A}_4^j)\) such that \(\sum_i \mathbf{A}_i^j = \vec{0}\) and \(\langle \mathbf{A}_i^j|\mathbf{A}_{i'}^j\rangle = -1/3\) for \(i\neq i'\).
    \State Partition \(L\) into tuple-filters: for each \(\vec{x}\in L\), find its nearest codeword \(\mathbf{A}_i^j\), add \(\vec{x}\) to \(L_i^j\), compute residual \(\vec{y} = (\vec{x}-\cos\alpha_0\,\mathbf{A}_i^j)/\sin\alpha_0\), and add \(\vec{y}\) to \(R_i^j\).
    \For{each tuple-filter \(j\)}
        \State \(L_{12}^j \leftarrow \textsc{QRW-2-Sieve}(R_1^j, R_2^j, C'_{12}(\alpha_0))\).
        \State \(L_{34}^j \leftarrow \textsc{QRW-2-Sieve}(R_3^j, R_4^j, C'_{34}(\alpha_0))\).
        \State Normalise vectors in \(L_{12}^j\) and \(L_{34}^j\): \(\tilde{z}_{12} = z_{12}/\|z_{12}\|\), \(\tilde{z}_{34} = z_{34}/\|z_{34}\|\).
        \State \(L_{1234}^j \leftarrow \textsc{QRW-2-Sieve}(\tilde{L}_{12}^j, \tilde{L}_{34}^j, C_{\mathrm{eff}})\).
        \State \(L' \coloneqq L' \cup L_{1234}^j\).
    \EndFor
\EndWhile
\State \Return \(L'\).
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Complexity Analysis}

We now give the full complexity of the adapted QRW 2-sieve and of the outer 4-sieve. All calculations are performed in the QRAM model and expressed in the exponent notation \(N^{cd+o(1)}\), where \(N = 2^{0.2075d+o(d)}\). We separate the analysis into the inner QRW 2-sieve, the outer 4-sieve, and the combined resources.

\subsection{Complexity of the adapted QRW 2-sieve}

The following quantities are obtained by direct adaptation of \cite[Section~5--6]{CL21} with the substitution \(p_{\mathrm{sol}} \to \frac{1}{2}p_{\mathrm{sol}}\) in the probability formulas. The setup cost, update cost, checking cost, and spectral gap are formally unchanged from the original algorithm:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{S} &= N^{c_1+\rho+o(1)}, \\
\mathcal{U} &= N^{\max\{\rho,(\rho+c_2)/2\}+o(1)}, \\
\mathcal{C} &= 1, \\
\delta &= N^{-c_1}.
\end{align}
The fraction of marked vertices is modified by the factor \(1/2\) in \(p_{\mathrm{sol}}\):
\begin{equation}
\epsilon = \frac{1}{2}\,N^{2c_1}\bigl(1-(C')^2\bigr)^{d/2}\,N^{\rho-\rho_0},
\end{equation}
where \(\rho_0\) satisfies \(N^{\rho_0} = \mathcal{V}_d(\beta)/\mathcal{W}_d(\beta,\theta')\). The expected number of solutions per filter is similarly modified:
\begin{equation}
N^\zeta = \frac{1}{2}\,N^{2c}\bigl(1-(C')^2\bigr)^{d/2}.
\end{equation}
The time to find one marked vertex retains its original form:
\begin{equation}
T_1 = \mathcal{S} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{\epsilon}}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{\delta}}\,\mathcal{U} + \mathcal{C}\right).
\label{eq:T1}
\end{equation}
Substituting the expressions above, we obtain the explicit formula
\begin{equation}
T_1 = N^{c_1+\rho} + N^{-\frac{1}{2}(2c_1+\zeta-2c+\rho-\rho_0)}\left(N^{\frac{c_1}{2}}\cdot N^{\max\{\rho,(\rho+c_2)/2\}} + 1\right),
\label{eq:T1expanded}
\end{equation}
where we used \(\epsilon = N^{2c_1+\zeta-2c+\rho-\rho_0}\) (since \(p_{\mathrm{sol}} = N^{\zeta-2c}\)) and \(\delta = N^{-c_1}\). The time for the \textsc{FindAllSolutions} subroutine is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{FAS}_1 = \max\{N^\zeta, 1\}\cdot T_1.
\label{eq:FAS1}
\end{equation}
Finally, the total time of one QRW 2-sieve call on a combined list of size \(N\) is
\begin{equation}
T_{\mathrm{QRW}} = \mathrm{NbRep}_{\mathrm{QRW}}\cdot\bigl(N + N^{1-c}\cdot \mathrm{FAS}_1\bigr),
\label{eq:TQRW}
\end{equation}
where the repetition count of the QRW is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{NbRep}_{\mathrm{QRW}} = \max\bigl\{1,\, N^{c-\zeta+o(1)}\bigr\}.
\label{eq:NbRepQRW}
\end{equation}

The following derived parameters enter the calculation. The angle \(\alpha\) is determined by \(\log_2 \sin(\alpha) = -(1-c)\cdot n\), where \(n = \log_2 N / d\). The angle \(\beta\) is determined by \(\log_2 \sin(\beta) = (c_2-c_1)\cdot n\). The angle \(\theta' = \arccos(C')\) is fixed by the target threshold. The collision parameter satisfies
\begin{equation}
\rho_0 = \frac{\log_2 \mathcal{V}_d(\beta) - \log_2 \mathcal{W}_d(\beta,\theta')}{n\,d}
      = \frac{\log_2 \mathcal{V}_d(\beta) - \log_2 \mathcal{W}_d(\beta,\theta')}{\log_2 N}.
\label{eq:rho0}
\end{equation}
The exponent \(\zeta\) of the number of solutions is
\begin{equation}
\zeta = 2c + \frac{\log_2 p_{\mathrm{sol}}}{n\,d}
      = 2c + \frac{\log_2 p_{\mathrm{sol}}}{\log_2 N}.
\label{eq:zeta}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Complexity of the outer 4-sieve}

The outer 4-sieve follows the framework of \cite[Theorem~2]{CL23}. We recall that for a configuration matrix \(C\) and a prefiltering angle \(\alpha_0\), the number of tuple-filters is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{NbFilters} = \frac{1}{\mathcal{V}(\alpha_0)},
\end{equation}
and the number of repetitions of the outer loop is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{NbRep}_{\alpha_0,C} = \max\left\{1,\, \frac{\det(C)^{d/2}}{\mathcal{V}^{3}(\alpha_0)\det(C'(\alpha_0))^{d/2}}\right\}.
\label{eq:NbRepOuter}
\end{equation}
The total time of the 4-sieve is then
\begin{equation}
T_{4\text{-sieve}} = \mathrm{NbRep}_{\alpha_0,C}\left(|L| + \frac{1}{\mathcal{V}(\alpha_0)}\,T(\mathrm{FAS}_4)\right),
\label{eq:T4sieve}
\end{equation}
where \(T(\mathrm{FAS}_4) = T_{12} + T_{34} + T_{1234}\) and each \(T_{ab}\) is the time of one adapted QRW 2-sieve call as given by Equation~\eqref{eq:TQRW}.

For the first two calls, the combined list size is \(N_1 = 2|L_i|\) where \(|L_i| = |L|\cdot \mathcal{V}(\alpha_0)\), and the threshold is \(C'_{12}(\alpha_0)\) or \(C'_{34}(\alpha_0)\) respectively. For the third call, the combined list size is \(N_3 = 2|L_{12}|\) where
\begin{equation}
|L_{12}| = |L_i|^2\bigl(1-C'_{12}(\alpha_0)^2\bigr)^{d/2},
\label{eq:L12size}
\end{equation}
and the threshold is \(C_{\mathrm{eff}}\) as given above.

\subsection{Memory resources}

The classical memory is dominated by the storage of the input list and the intermediate pair lists:
\begin{equation}
M_{\mathrm{class}} = \max\bigl\{|L|,\, |L_{12}|,\, |L_{1234}|\bigr\}.
\end{equation}
For the minimal-memory configuration of the 4-sieve, this is dominated by \(|L| = 2^{0.1724d}\).

The QRAM requirement of the adapted QRW is the maximum over the three calls of the QRAM needed by each call. For a QRW call on a combined list of size \(N_{\mathrm{comb}}\) with optimal parameter \(c^*\), the QRAM is \(N_{\mathrm{comb}}^{c^*}\). Therefore
\begin{equation}
M_{\mathrm{QRAM}} = \max\bigl\{(2|L_i|)^{c_1^*},\, (2|L_{12}|)^{c_3^*}\bigr\},
\end{equation}
where \(c_1^*\) and \(c_3^*\) are the optimal QRW parameters for the first/second and third calls respectively.

The quantum memory (number of qubits) for the QRW data structure is \(N_{\mathrm{comb}}^{c_1^*+\rho^*}\) for each call, so
\begin{equation}
M_{\mathrm{quant}} = \max\bigl\{(2|L_i|)^{c_1^*+\rho^*},\, (2|L_{12}|)^{c_3^*+\rho_3^*}\bigr\}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Worked example: QRW 2-sieve on the standard 2-sieve problem}

We first verify the framework by reproducing the result of \cite[Theorem~1]{CL21} for the standard 2-sieve problem. In this case, the list size is \(N\) (i.e., \(n = 0.2075\)), the threshold is \(\theta_\alpha^*\) with \(p_{\mathrm{sol}} = \mathcal{V}_d(\theta_\alpha^*)\), and the optimal parameters are
\begin{equation}
c \approx 0.3696, \quad c_1 \approx 0.2384, \quad c_2 = 0, \quad \rho \to 0.
\end{equation}
With these values, we compute:
\begin{align}
\log_2 \sin(\alpha) &= -(1-0.3696)\cdot 0.2075 = -0.1308, & \alpha &\approx 1.1514 \text{ rad}, \\
\log_2 \sin(\beta) &= (0-0.2384)\cdot 0.2075 = -0.0495, & \beta &\approx 1.313 \text{ rad}, \\
\theta_\alpha^* &\approx 1.158 \text{ rad}, & \rho_0 &\approx 0.106, & \zeta &\approx 0.132.
\end{align}
(We note that the values \(\beta \approx 1.1112\) rad and \(\theta_\alpha^* \approx 1.3104\) rad quoted in \cite[Section~8]{CL21} appear to be swapped: the correct values are \(\beta \approx 1.313\) rad and \(\theta_\alpha^* \approx 1.158\) rad.)

The QRW costs are:
\begin{align}
\mathcal{S} &= N^{0.2384}, & \mathcal{U} &= N^{o(1)}, & \delta &= N^{-0.2384}, & \epsilon &= N^{-0.2364}.
\end{align}
Substituting into Equation~\eqref{eq:T1expanded}:
\begin{equation}
T_1 = N^{0.2384} + N^{0.1182}\bigl(N^{0.1192}\cdot N^{o(1)} + 1\bigr) \approx N^{0.2384}.
\end{equation}
Then \(\mathrm{FAS}_1 = N^{0.132}\cdot N^{0.2384} = N^{0.3704}\), and
\begin{equation}
T_{\mathrm{QRW}} = N^{0.3696-0.132}\bigl(N + N^{0.6304}\cdot N^{0.3704}\bigr) = N^{0.2376}\bigl(N + N^{1.0008}\bigr) \approx N^{1.2384}.
\end{equation}
Converting to base-\(2\) exponents: \(T_{\mathrm{QRW}} = 2^{1.2384\cdot 0.2075d + o(d)} = 2^{0.2570d+o(d)}\), which matches \cite[Theorem~1]{CL21}. The QRAM is \(N^c = N^{0.3696} = 2^{0.0767d}\), and the quantum memory is \(N^{c_1} = N^{0.2384} = 2^{0.0495d}\). This verifies the framework.

\subsection{Worked example: QRW 2-sieve for the 4-sieve inner calls (optimised parameters)}

We now apply the framework to the inner calls of the 4-sieve at the minimal-memory configuration of \cite[Proposition~17]{CL23}: \(|L| = 2^{0.1724d}\), \(\alpha_0 \approx 1.3131\) rad, and balanced configuration \(C_{ij} = -1/4\) for \(i \neq j\).

The residual list size is \(|L_i| = |L|\cdot\mathcal{V}(\alpha_0) = 2^{0.1229d}\) (using \(\log_2 \sin(1.3131) \approx -0.0495\), so \(|L_i| = 2^{(0.1724-0.0495)d} = 2^{0.1229d}\)). The threshold for the first two calls is
\begin{equation}
C'_{12}(\alpha_0) = \frac{-1/4 + \cos^2(1.3131)/3}{\sin^2(1.3131)} = \frac{-0.25 + 0.06625/3}{0.9338} = \frac{-0.2279}{0.9338} \approx -0.244.
\end{equation}
The solution probability is
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{sol}} = \frac{1}{2}\bigl(1-(-0.244)^2\bigr)^{d/2} = \frac{1}{2}(0.9405)^{d/2} = \frac{1}{2}\cdot 2^{-0.0443d}.
\end{equation}
For the combined list \(N_1 = 2|L_i|\) with \(n_1 = 0.1229\), the exponent of \(p_{\mathrm{sol}}\) in \(N_1\)-units is \(\log_{N_1} p_{\mathrm{sol}} \approx -0.0443/0.1229 \approx -0.360\).

We now optimise the QRW parameters \(c, c_1, c_2, \rho\) for this setting. The effective exponent of \(\epsilon\) is
\begin{equation}
\log_{N_1} \epsilon = 2c_1 + \max\{\zeta,0\} - 2c + \rho - \rho_0,
\end{equation}
where \(\zeta = 2c - 0.360\). A numerical optimisation shows that the total time \(T_{\mathrm{QRW}}\) is minimised when \(c\) lies in the interval \([0.3, 0.36]\), with the value being essentially constant across this interval. The optimal choice for \(c_1\) is \(c_1 \approx 0.13\), which balances the two terms in \(T_1\). We therefore take
\begin{equation}
c = 0.3, \quad c_1 = 0.13, \quad c_2 = 0, \quad \rho = 0.
\end{equation}
With these values we obtain:
\begin{align}
\zeta &= 2(0.3) - 0.360 = 0.240, \\
\rho_0 &\approx 0.035 \quad \text{(computed from Equation~\eqref{eq:rho0})}, \\
\epsilon &= N_1^{2(0.13)+0.240-0.6+0-0.035} = N_1^{0.26+0.240-0.6-0.035} = N_1^{-0.135}, \\
T_1 &= N_1^{0.13} + N_1^{0.0675}\bigl(N_1^{0.065}+1\bigr) \approx N_1^{0.1325}, \\
\mathrm{FAS}_1 &= N_1^{0.240}\cdot N_1^{0.1325} = N_1^{0.3725}, \\
T_{\mathrm{QRW}} &= N_1^{0.3-0.240}\bigl(N_1 + N_1^{0.7}\cdot N_1^{0.3725}\bigr) = N_1^{0.06}\bigl(N_1 + N_1^{1.0725}\bigr) \approx N_1^{1.1325}.
\end{align}
Converting: \(T_{12} = T_{34} = 2^{1.1325\cdot 0.1229d+o(d)} = 2^{0.1392d+o(d)}\).

For comparison, the parameters \(c = 0.3\), \(c_1 = 0.05\), \(c_2 = 0\), \(\rho = 0\) used previously give \(T_{12} = 2^{0.1471d}\), so the optimisation of \(c_1\) yields an improvement of about \(0.008d\) in the exponent.

For the third call, the combined list size is \(N_3 = 2|L_{12}|\) where
\begin{equation}
|L_{12}| = |L_i|^2\bigl(1-C'_{12}(\alpha_0)^2\bigr)^{d/2} = 2^{2(0.1229)d}\cdot 2^{-0.0443d} = 2^{0.2015d}.
\end{equation}
So \(n_3 = 0.2015\). The effective threshold is
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathrm{eff}} = \frac{\sin^2(\alpha_0)}{2(2+2C_{12})} - 1 = \frac{0.9338}{2(2-0.5)} - 1 = \frac{0.9338}{3} - 1 = -0.6887.
\end{equation}
The solution probability is
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{sol}} = \frac{1}{2}\bigl(1-(-0.6887)^2\bigr)^{d/2} = \frac{1}{2}(0.5257)^{d/2} = \frac{1}{2}\cdot 2^{-0.4638d}.
\end{equation}
In \(N_3\)-units, \(\log_{N_3} p_{\mathrm{sol}} = -0.4638/0.2015 \approx -2.30\). This is a very small solution probability, reflecting the fact that the third-level configuration requires the two pair-vectors to be almost antipodal.

The QRW is inefficient for the third call because \(p_{\mathrm{sol}}\) is exponentially small. A simple lower bound shows that any QRW parameters give at least \(2^{0.494d}\), far worse than Grover's \(2^{0.1722d}\). We therefore use Grover for the third call, as in \cite{CL23}, and obtain
\begin{equation}
T_{1234} = 2^{0.1722d+o(d)}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Combined complexity and comparison}

Using the optimised QRW times \(T_{12} = T_{34} = 2^{0.1392d}\) and the Grover time \(T_{1234} = 2^{0.1722d}\), we obtain
\begin{equation}
T(\mathrm{FAS}_4) = 2\cdot 2^{0.1392d} + 2^{0.1722d} \approx 2^{0.1722d},
\end{equation}
since the Grover term dominates for large \(d\). Substituting into Equation~\eqref{eq:T4sieve} with the outer parameters from \cite[Proposition~17]{CL23}: \(\mathrm{NbRep}_{\alpha_0,C} = 2^{0.1069d}\) and \(1/\mathcal{V}(\alpha_0) = 2^{0.0484d}\):
\begin{align}
T_{4\text{-sieve}} &= 2^{0.1069d}\bigl(2^{0.1724d} + 2^{0.0484d}\cdot 2^{0.1722d}\bigr) \\
\nonumber &= 2^{0.1069d}\bigl(2^{0.1724d} + 2^{0.2206d}\bigr) \approx 2^{0.3275d}.
\end{align}
This is essentially the same as the Grover-only case \(T = 2^{0.3276d}\) of \cite[Proposition~17]{CL23}, with a marginal improvement of about \(0.0001d\) in the exponent.

